=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Recognize reciprocal chromosome translocations.
- Distinguish balanced and unbalanced chromosome products.
- Relate translocations to semisterility and disease.
- Identify chromosome changes in the worked examples.

=== layout: section

# Chromosome Abnormalities: Translocations

=== layout: one-panel

# *Alterations of Chromosome Structure*

@body

- Breakage of a chromosome can lead to four types of changes in chromosome structure
  - [*Deletion*]{underline=true} removes a chromosomal segment
  - [*Duplication*]{underline=true} repeats a segment
  - [*Inversion*]{underline=true} reverses orientation of a segment within a chromosome
  - [*Translocation*]{color=red underline=true} [moves a segment from one chromosome to another]{color=red}

=== layout: section

# Translocations

=== layout: one-panel

# *Translocation*

@body

![Slide image 1](assets/lect13c-chromo_alter_4-translocation/translocation_overview.drawn.svg)

A translocation moves a segment from one chromosome to a nonhomologous chromosome.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Translocations*

@body

![Slide image 2](assets/lect13c-chromo_alter_4-translocation/translocation-complements.drawn.svg)

- (A) Two pairs of non-homologous chromosomes in a diploid organism.
- (B) Heterozygous reciprocal translocation, in which two non-homologous chromosomes (the two at the top) have interchanged terminal segments.
- (C) Homozygous reciprocal translocation.

=== layout: two-panels
@replaceme

# *Semi-sterility*

@left

![Slide image 3](assets/lect13c-chromo_alter_4-translocation/translocation-heterozygote.drawn.svg)

![Slide image 4](assets/lect13c-chromo_alter_4-translocation/balanced-four-gametes.drawn.svg)

![Slide image 5](assets/lect13c-chromo_alter_4-translocation/image_005.png)

@right

- half the gametes will be incapable of contributing to the next generation, a condition known as semisterility or &#8220;half sterility.&#8221;
  - In plants, the 50 percent unbalanced meiotic products from segregation generally abort at the gametic stage.
  - In animals, however, the duplication&#8211;deletion products are viable as gametes but lethal to the zygote.

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Disorders Caused by Translocations*

@body

![Slide image 6](assets/lect13c-chromo_alter_4-translocation/blood-cell-comparison.drawn.svg)

- Certain cancers, including chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML), are caused by translocations of chromosomes

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme


@body

![Slide image 7](assets/lect13c-chromo_alter_4-translocation/philadelphia-chromosome.drawn.svg)

- *Normal chromosome 9*

- *Normal chromosome 22*

- *Reciprocal translocation*

- *Translocated chromosome 9*

- *Translocated chromosome 22 (Philadelphia chromosome)*

- The cancerous cells in nearly all Chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML) patients contain\:
  - an abnormally short [chromosome 22]{color=purple}, the so-called Philadelphia chromosome, and
  - an abnormally long chromosome 9.
- These altered chromosomes result from the reciprocal translocation shown here,
  - which presumably occurred in a single white blood cell precursor undergoing mitosis and was then passed along to all descendant cells.

